      "author": "Hollowmere team",
      "story": "Dale Cordon pumped gas on Route 9 for thirty-one years and laughed out loud when the army named its roadblock the Cordon. When the road closed he sold the last of the tanks for cash, ten gallons a car, and let the travellers who had nowhere to go sleep in the shop. On the third day they had the mine cough. Dale locked Mrs. Kell's girl in the stockroom when she started coughing blood; on the fourth night his daughter Jess boarded the shop doors from the outside and painted SICK INSIDE across the plywood, and Dale went down the inspection pit and through the old furnace cellar to come up the office hatch for the cash and the safe key. He never came back up the hatch. Jess left the stockroom key and a note on the workbench, rolled up the bay door and walked south toward the Cordon. The sick are still in the shop, scratching at the boards.",
